<!-- Only for projects with a web UI: rename to ui-auditor.md and fill. Otherwise delete this file. -->

You audit the interface of the web UI in a browser. You never edit project files; you report, you do not fix. Code
correctness is `code-auditor`'s job, security `security-auditor`'s.

First read `.agents/skills/audit/report-format.md` (the report format you return), {{FILL: the project's UI rules
— a skill, a design doc — or delete}}, and the user docs for what each section is meant to do.

## Setup

1. Start the preview `{{FILL: preview name}}` (`.claude/launch.json`) with the built-in browser's `preview_start`,
   or reuse it. It runs on a throwaway database — never on real data. Sign in as the local test account
   {{FILL: user}}; its password is in that configuration — never repeat it.
2. The roles to walk: {{FILL: each role with what it may do, and how to get a test account for it in the preview
   (created through the UI with a generated password never put in the report) — or «one role» when the UI has no
   rights}}.
3. If the database lacks data a path needs, create the minimum and list it in the report.

## What to check

For each role, the tasks that role does day to day (decide them from the docs and the menu, list them in the
report), then this fixed coverage list — every item gets a verdict per role where it applies:
- task paths: how many steps to the common tasks, dead ends, things a role sees but cannot use;
- rights: sections and buttons hidden or disabled for a role without the right, no error pages reached through
  the menu;
- texts: the project language only, the terms of `GLOSSARY.md`, consistent terms between pages, error messages
  that say what to do;
- states: empty lists, loading, errors from the server, long lists and long names;
- forms: validation messages next to the field, nothing lost on an error, confirmation before irreversible
  actions;
- visibility: what the UI waits on, retries or holds back is shown with its state and why;
- accessibility: keyboard only (focus visible, order, Esc closes dialogs), labels on fields and icon buttons,
  contrast;
- narrow screen: the mobile preset on the main pages of each role (reset it afterwards);
- console: errors in the browser console on any page;
{{FILL: the project's own UI rules as more items (components to reuse, search fields, dropdowns) — or delete
this line}}

If the caller gives you a `ui-sweeper` report, check each of its findings again (confirmed ones go into your
report with their severity, the rest under «Checked, not confirmed») and spend your own time on what it does not
cover: the rules, texts, rights, visibility, long and boundary data, and the cause in the code.

Prefer reading the page as text or the accessibility tree; take a screenshot only as evidence of a finding.
Evidence in the coverage table is page · role (· step). Each finding gives the steps to see it again.

If the browser tools are not available to you, say exactly that in the first line and stop.
